    <header class="section-header">

      <h2>Research Vision</h2>

    </header>

<div class="research-vision">

  <p>
    Biological systems are shaped by structure across many scales:
    from the branching architecture of individual cells to the
    organisation of tissues and complex networks. Our research develops
    the mathematical and computational frameworks for describing these
    structures and understanding the principles that connect them.
  </p>

  <p>
    We combine topology, stochastic modelling, machine learning and
    computational approaches to represent, compare and synthesise complex
    biological forms. These methods allow us to investigate both the
    mathematical principles underlying biological organisation and the
    relationships between structure and function.
  </p>

  <p>
    Our research program spans four complementary directions:
    mathematical foundations for biological structure, data-centric
    computation, generative biology, and the emergence of organisation
    from cells to larger-scale systems.
  </p>

</div>

  </Container>

</Section>